<BlogPost title="Travelling Slovenia With a Dog: A Calm Road-Trip Guide | Nani Pup" description="A practical guide to travelling Slovenia with a dog: accommodation, driving, heat, alpine days, routines, rest and when private dog care can help." canonical="/journal/travelling-slovenia-with-dog/" alternateEn="/journal/travelling-slovenia-with-dog/" alternateSl="/sl/blog/potovanje-po-sloveniji-s-psom/" eyebrow="SLOVENIA ROAD TRIP" heading="Travelling Slovenia with a dog, <em>without making every day a dog mission.</em>" dek="A good dog-friendly trip is not about proving your dog can come everywhere. It is about building a rhythm that works for both of you." related={[{href:'/journal/dog-friendly-bled/',label:'Dog-Friendly Bled'},{href:'/journal/dog-friendly-kranjska-gora/',label:'Kranjska Gora with a dog'}]} ctaTitle="Build one easy dog-free window into the trip." ctaText="Private care around Gorenjska can make demanding hikes, spa time, transfers and long event days much easier for both of you." ctaHref="/dog-sitting-gorenjska/" ctaLabel="Private care in Gorenjska →">
<p>Slovenia is compact, which makes it ideal for a road trip with a dog. Lakes, towns, valleys and the coast can fit into one itinerary — but frequent location changes can be more tiring for dogs than owners realise.</p>
<h2>Book the dog, not only the hotel</h2>
<p>“Pets allowed” is only the first question. Check whether your dog may stay alone in the room, whether there is safe outdoor space nearby, where the nearest shaded walk is and what happens at breakfast or dinner.</p>
<h2>Protect the routine</h2>
<p>Keep at least a few anchors from home: familiar food, the same bed or blanket, predictable meal times and some genuinely quiet hours. New places are enrichment, but constant novelty can become stress.</p>
<h2>Do not stack the hardest days</h2>
<p>A long mountain day followed by a crowded city and then a five-hour drive might be a great holiday schedule for humans and a poor one for a dog. Alternate high-stimulation days with slower ones.</p>
<h2>Heat matters more than the itinerary</h2>
<p>Summer in valleys and cities can be much warmer than expected from alpine travel photos. Shift walks to mornings and evenings, protect paws from hot surfaces and never build the day around leaving a dog in a parked car.</p>
<h2>Use care as part of the trip</h2>
<p>Private dog sitting is not a failure of dog-friendly travel. It can be the reason the whole trip works: your dog gets a calm half-day while you visit a spa, attend a wedding, take a difficult hike or enjoy a long meal.</p>
<div class="noteBox"><strong>Nani idea:</strong> give your dog one “nothing day” on a multi-day road trip — familiar walks, naps, sniffing and no itinerary.</div>
<p>Nani Pup’s core service area is Lake Bled and Gorenjska, including selected Kranjska Gora bookings, with limited care in Ljubljana.</p>
